    {
      "local_id": 220,
      "chapter_id": 32,
      "topic": "",
      "type": "sba",
      "source_number": "003",
      "lead_in": "",
      "stem": "A 46-year-old man presents with a 6-month history of dyspeptic symptoms despite regular use of pantoprazole. On upper gastrointestinal endoscopy and biopsy and a rapid urease test, he is found to have a Helicobacter pylori infection. If left untreated over the long term, which one of the following conditions is most likely to develop?",
      "options": [
        "Acute gastric ulcer",
        "Acute duodenal ulcer",
        "Gastric lymphoma",
        "Hiatus hernia",
        "Oesophageal carcinoma"
      ],
      "answer": "C",
      "answer_source": "book_key",
      "explanation": "This patient might develop a gastric lymphoma if left untreated. Gastric mucosa-associated lymphoid tumour (MALToma) occurs in long-standing Helicobacter pylori chronic gastritis which provides the immunological stimulus for B cell proliferation. There are several causes of gastritis (Figure 46.5) of which H. pylori is one.",
      "images": [],
      "explanation_images": [],
      "group_id": "",
      "no_shuffle": false,
      "source_page": 124,
      "source_pages": [
