    {
      "local_id": 222,
      "chapter_id": 32,
      "topic": "",
      "type": "sba",
      "source_number": "005",
      "lead_in": "",
      "stem": "A 19-year-old man is admitted with a 2-day history of right-sided abdominal pain, tenderness in the right iliac fossa and raised inflammatory markers. Following a clinical diagnosis of acute appendicitis, he is laparoscoped prior to his proposed appendicectomy. At laparoscopy, a 10 cm segment of the terminal ileum appears red, inflamed and thickened with free blood-stained fluid in the pelvis. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
      "options": [
        "Acute appendicitis",
        "Acute intestinal ischaemia",
        "Crohn’s disease",
        "Intussusception",
        "Primary intestinal lymphoma"
      ],
      "answer": "C",
      "answer_source": "book_key",
      "explanation": "The most likely cause of this appearance is Crohn’s disease, also called regional enteritis. The condition is a chronic transmural inflammatory disease that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract.",
